#	$NetBSD$
#
# HPT5325 -- HP T5325 thin client, Marvell 88F6281 (Kirkwood/"Orion")
#
# Hardware facts were read from the running device over SSH, plus the U-Boot
# and Linux boot logs and a known-good NetBSD boot of this exact board.  The
# survey notes and raw captures live outside this tree, in the project's
# NOTES.md and notes/raw/.
#
# SoC      Marvell 88F6281 A1 LE (Kirkwood), /proc/cpuinfo=Feroceon-KW
# Board    DB-88F6281A-BP LE (Linux reports "OpenRD-Base")
# CPU      ARM926EJ-S rev 1, ARMv5TE, single core (NetBSD: "Sheeva 88SV131")
# FPU      none -- soft-float evbarm-earmv5, not -hf
# Clocks   CPU 1200 MHz, SysClock 400, TClock 200
# RAM      512 MB = 2x256MB DDR2, CS0 0x00000000 / CS1 0x10000000, 16-bit bus
# Flash    1MB SPI NOR @0xf4000000 (U-Boot 512K, SSD fw 256K, 3x64K env)
# Disk     SATA port 0 = SM224 486MB (on-board); port 1 = ADATA SP310 60GB
# Net      GbE port0, PHY at MDIO 8; port1 unused
# Video    XGI Volari Z11, PCI 18ca:0027, class 0x030000, 16MB VRAM
# Serial   2x16550A fitted (ttyS0 irq33, ttyS1 irq34); ttyS0 = system console
# USB      1x EHCI port; an SMSC USB2514 hub is fitted on it
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROVEN BASELINE -- match this unless you have a reason not to
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NetBSD 6.1.4 booted to a login prompt on this exact board and this exact
# U-Boot build (MIC 3.4.19-16).  Its log is the reference for every choice
# below.  It had no video driver, which is precisely why it showed
#
#   vendor 0x18ca product 0x0027 (VGA display) at pci0 dev 1 function 0
#       not configured
#
# and why xgiframe exists.  Everything else it reported we inherit unchanged:
#
#   cpu0 Sheeva 88SV131 rev 1 (ARM9E-S core)      -> options CPU_SHEEVA
#   mvsoc0 Marvell 88F6281 Rev. A1 Kirkwood       -> options KIRKWOOD
#   com0 offset 0x12000 irq 33 -> "com0: console"  -> automatic, see CONSOLE
#   mvsata0 GenIIe, 1hc, 2port/hc -> wd0, wd1     -> mvsata, ata, wd
#   mvgbe0 + ukphy0 at phy 8                      -> mvgbe, mii, ukphy
#   ehci0 irq 19 -> usb0, uhub0, uhub1            -> ehci, usb, uhub
#   gttwsi0 irq 29 -> iic0                        -> gttwsi, iic
#   mvpex0 irq 9 -> pci0                          -> mvpex, pci
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOAD ADDRESS -- do not override; the default is the proven one
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# sys/arch/evbarm/conf/mk.marvell already defaults to
#
#     KERNEL_BASE_PHYS ?= 0x00008000
#     KERNEL_BASE_VIRT ?= 0xc0008000
#
# and the 2014 kernel booted with exactly those: its uImage header read
# "Load Address: 00008000 / Entry Point: 00008000".  So there is no need for
# a makeoptions override here, and adding one only creates a way to drift away
# from a configuration that is known to work.
#
# The U-Boot banner says "Addresses 8M - 0M are saved for the U-Boot usage",
# which is why an earlier revision of this file pushed the kernel up to 8MB.
# That is over-cautious: the banner is a reservation policy, not a map of what
# U-Boot occupies.  What it actually places is
#
#     U-Boot code  0x00600000 - 0x0067FFF0,  BSS -> 0x006CD4E0
#     malloc pool  0x00700000 - 0x00800000      ("(8M - 7M)")
#
# leaving 0x00000000 - 0x00600000 free apart from the Marvell boot monitor in
# the first 32KB.  A ~4MB kernel at 0x00008000 ends around 0x00420000, well
# clear of U-Boot.  (OPENRD, the upstream sibling config, does use 8MB; that
# works too, but it is not what is proven on this board.)
#
# Because MKUBOOTIMAGEARGS in mk.marvell uses $(KERNEL_BASE_PHYS), the uImage
# the build produces carries the right load/entry addresses automatically --
# do NOT hand-wrap it with mkimage.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ROOT DEVICE -- not compiled in, comes from the U-Boot command line
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NetBSD reads "root=" from the bootargs Marvell's bootm passes in r3:
#   sys/arch/evbarm/marvell/marvell_machdep.c -> parse_mi_bootargs
#   -> set_root_device() in sys/arch/evbarm/evbarm/autoconf.c
#
# There is no BOOT_ARGS fallback on this path (that option only exists for the
# fdt/gemini/kobo machines), so root must be passed at boot or NetBSD will
# guess.  The 2014 boot passed nothing and landed on:
#
#     root on wd1a dumps on wd1b
#
# i.e. the 60GB ADATA SP310 on SATA *port 1*, not the 486MB SM224 on port 0.
# Pass it explicitly:
#
#     bootm <addr> root=wd1a
#
# Marvell's own U-Boot convention is "rootdev=mgi0", which NetBSD ignores -- it
# falls through silently and can pick the wrong disk.  "mgi0" is also
# Marvell's Linux-side name; the NetBSD interface is mvgbe0.
#
# NOTE: U-Boot loads the kernel itself from the *other* partition of that same
# disk ("fatload ide 1:1"), while NetBSD roots on partition "a" of it.  Confirm
# the on-disk partition layout before the first boot -- if U-Boot is reading
# p1 as FAT and NetBSD is asked for wd1a as ffs, one of the two is wrong.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONSOLE -- keep BOTH; serial is how we debug this driver
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# com0 is attached as the system console unconditionally: the local consinit()
# in sys/arch/evbarm/marvell/marvell_machdep.c calls mvuart_cnattach() for
# MVSOC_COM0_BASE before any autoconfiguration, at CONSPEED (115200 8N1).  No
# config line is needed to get it, and the 2014 log shows "com0: console".
#
# xgiframe then calls wsdisplay_cnattach(), which takes cn_tab over for video
# and stashes the previous console in wsdisplay_ocn.  WSDISPLAY_MULTICONS is
# what makes that useful: wsdisplay_cnputc() mirrors every character to
# wsdisplay_ocn as well, and wsdisplay_getc() falls back to it for input.  So
# with it enabled we get video *and* a serial transcript of the same session --
# the only way to tell a failed mode set from a working one, and the only way
# to reach the DDB prompt if video is not up yet.
#
# CONSPEED is stated explicitly below only to document the rate; the value
# matches both the built-in B115200 default and U-Boot's own setting.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VIDEO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# XGI Volari Z11, PCI 18ca:0027, class 0x030000.  Device ID is known to NetBSD
# as PCI_PRODUCT_XGI_VOLARI_Z11 (sys/dev/pci/pcidevs.h).
#
# BAR2 (0x1000-0x107f) is PCI I/O space holding the legacy VGA register window,
# relocated to BAR2+0x30 -- Marvell's own driver prints "XGIfb: Relocate IO
# address: 1000 [00001030]", which matches.  BAR0 (0xe8000000, 16MB) is the
# linear framebuffer; BAR1 (0xec000000, 256KB) is MMIO that xgifb only ever
# reports and never accesses, so xgiframe does not map it.
#
# Registers are programmed through the legacy index/data pairs; there is no
# VGA-compatible BIOS POST on ARM (VGA_POST is x86-only), which is why the
# generic vga driver cannot do this and xgiframe exists.
#
# Default mode 0x4a is 1024x768x16 (Marvell's driver reports "Default mode is
# 1024x768x16 (60Hz)"), hence 16bpp RGB565 and a 2048-byte stride.
#
# Do NOT list vga* here as well: vga_pci_match() would also bind on PCI class
# code, and both drivers would attach to the same device and fight over it.
#
# Device lines come last on purpose: config(5) preserves declaration order into
# ioconf.c, and "attach ... at pci?" is only resolved because SHEEVAPLUG has
# already declared pci* above us.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mvsdio (Marvell SD/MMC) does not work on OPENRD-family boards -- the 2014 log
# shows "mvsdio at mvsoc0 unit 0 not configured" -- but "no mvsdio" is NOT
# valid config syntax: "no" accepts options / makeoptions / file-system, not a
# device name, and nbconfig fails with a syntax error.  Excluding a driver
# SHEEVAPLUG already pulled in needs an edit to the parent config, which an
# include cannot do.  Left enabled; it never attaches.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

include		"arch/evbarm/conf/SHEEVAPLUG"

# Board id.  Purely cosmetic here -- the only functional use on Marvell is the
# uImage name (-n "NetBSD/$(BOARDTYPE) ..."), and the gtpci interrupt-pin hint
# table is #if defined(ORION), which Kirkwood does not set.  Match the 2014
# kernel, whose image was named "NetBSD/hpt5325 6.1.4".
#
# SHEEVAPLUG sets both of these already, so clear them first or nbconfig warns
# "already have makeoptions `BOARDTYPE=sheevaplug'".
no makeoptions	BOARDTYPE
makeoptions	BOARDTYPE="hpt5325"
no options	EVBARM_BOARDTYPE
options		EVBARM_BOARDTYPE=hpt5325

# No load-address overrides on purpose -- see LOAD ADDRESS above; the stock
# mk.marvell defaults (0x00008000 / 0xc0008000) are the proven ones.

# Verbose init: with serial we have a real console, so early output is cheap
# and worth having while bringing up new hardware.
options		MVSOC_CONSOLE_EARLY
options		VERBOSE_INIT_ARM

options		WSDISPLAY_MULTICONS
options		CONSPEED=115200

# No FPU on this SoC; the soft-float build is correct.
#options		FPU_VFP

xgiframe*	at pci? dev ? function ?
wsdisplay*	at xgiframe? console ?
